    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-11822",
      "knowledge_point_id": "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-4)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+4) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
