      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/assets/2819ceb4214f8df19ef349794c243771a1080bccf0bf2bbdbdbca9be67d4fbdb.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,6).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-6).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (4+4)^{2} & =4p(-6-6) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
